      {
        "id": "lem-finite-sampling-reconstruction-bridge-for-neural-operator-quadratures",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Finite sampling reconstruction bridges compact-uniform raw quadrature limits",
        "deps": [
          "def-discretization-invariant-operator-family",
          "lem-neural-operator-compact-union-under-uniform-convergence",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "thm-heine-cantor-metric",
          "thm-compact-implies-complete-and-totally-bounded"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "statement": "Let $X,U$ be real Banach spaces, and let continuous maps $H,H_n:X\\to U$ satisfy $\\sup_{a\\in K}\\|H_n(a)-H(a)\\|_U\\to0$ for every norm-compact $K\\subset X$. Suppose continuous finite-dimensional sampling and reconstruction maps $S_n:X\\to E_n$ and $J_n:E_n\\to X$ are supplied and satisfy $\\sup_{a\\in K}\\|J_nS_n(a)-a\\|_X\\to0$ on every such $K$. Then\n$$G_n(a)=H_n(J_n(S_n(a)))$$\nis a finite-sampling family in the displayed definition, with $F_n=E_n$, finite map the identity and output reconstruction $R_n=H_n\\circ J_n:E_n\\to U$, and $G_n\\to H$ uniformly on every norm-compact input set in the actual $U$ norm.\n\nFor the supremum-norm continuous-input case these reconstruction hypotheses always hold with exactly the prescribed tags of arbitrary finite partitions of a nonempty bounded open $D\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ whose maximal cell diameters $h_n$ tend to zero. Namely let $X=C(\\bar D;\\mathbb R^q)$, let $S_n(a)=(a(\\xi_{n,j}))_j$, and, when $h_n>0$, for $x\\in\\bar D$ put\n$$w_{n,j}(x)=\\max\\{0,2h_n-|x-\\xi_{n,j}|\\},\\qquad J_n(z)(x)=\\frac{\\sum_jw_{n,j}(x)z_j}{\\sum_jw_{n,j}(x)}.$$\nIf $h_n=0$, the domain is the zero-dimensional singleton; define $J_n(z)$ to be the constant function with value $z_1$. This reconstructs every input exactly. In both branches $\\|J_nS_n\\|_{X\\to X}\\le1$, and the reconstruction error is bounded by the input modulus of continuity at $2h_n$, uniformly on every compact input family. In a stronger Banach input norm the first clause applies whenever the stated reconstructions converge in that norm; convergence in the supremum norm alone is not substituted for that condition.\n\nA further case needs no input reconstruction. Suppose a raw finite architecture first uses its input only through the sampled pointwise lift in the integral-only field $v_{1,n}(x)=\\sum_j\\omega_{n,j}\\kappa_n(x,\\xi_{n,j})P(a(\\xi_{n,j}))+b_n(x)$, and every later stage acts on this field and the fixed parameters without further access to the original input. Assume these stages are defined and continuous on the compatible Banach spaces in question. Then each raw map itself factors through the finite input sample vector. Its compact-uniform raw convergence therefore already gives a finite-sampling family in the actual output norm, regardless of whether a sample reconstruction into the input norm has been supplied. This includes the integral-only universality subclass, when its kernels and intermediate spaces satisfy the declared compatible norm hypotheses."
      },
